#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179367 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/id #uuid "622bd0ff-d08a-5504-8b38-8bc388bfcbf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12525" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ acting in the direction $OP$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2$." :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x=x_1$ to $x=x_2$ is given by\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ at a rate of $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ is\n\n$$\nF = ma = 3 \\cdot (1 + 2x^2) = (3 + 6x^2) \\,\\rm{N}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= ∫_{0}^{2}(3 + 6x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= [3x + 2x^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3(2) + 2(2^{3}) - (3(0) + 2(0^{3})) \\\\\n&= 6 + 16 - 0 \\\\\n&= 22J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179368 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "4e428ee4-0017-5e83-887c-26e723b4af08" :question/math-academy-id "e-12526" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(4t+1)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$, where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x$, and $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}$." :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$, initial velocity $v_0$, and final velocity $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\nv(t) &= ∫(4t + 1)dt \\\\\nv(t) &= 2t^{2} + t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = 0$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 2(0^2) + 0 + C \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad C = 0\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t) = 2t^2 + t\n$$\n\nIn particular, its inital velocity at time\n\n$$\nt = 1\\,\\rm{s}\n$$\n\nand final velocity at time\n\n$$\nt = 2\\,\\rm{s}\n$$\n\nare\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{0} &= v(1) = 2(1^{2}) + 1 = 3m/s, \\\\\nv_{1} &= v(2) = 2(2^{2}) + 2 = 10m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔKE &= \\frac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 10^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3^{2} \\\\\n&= 100 - 9 \\\\\n&= 91J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179369 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/id #uuid "39eab788-1a8d-51af-b535-594cacc8ec2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12527" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(t^2-2t+3)\\,\\rm{m}$, where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t=3\\,\\rm{s}$." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=t^2-2t+3\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - 2t + 3) \\\\\n&= 2t - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\nv_0 = v(1) = 2(1) -2 = 0\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nand its final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\nv_1 = v(3) = 2(3) -2= 4\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= ΔKE \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot (4)^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 0^{2} \\\\\n&= 8 - 0 \\\\\n&= 8J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179370 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "8f79d7e9-377f-5012-a762-279839fc806a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12528" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(x+3)\\,\\rm{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2$, calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4$." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=2$ to $x=4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= ∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}F(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{2}^{4}(x + 3)dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + 3x]_{2}^{4} \\\\\n&= (8 + 12) - (2 + 6) \\\\\n&= 12J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity\n\n$$\nv=2\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nat $x=2$. So, we can find the velocity $v_1$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= \\frac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2} \\\\\n12 &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot v_{1}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2^{2} \\\\\n12 &= v_{1}^{2} - 4 \\\\\nv_{1}^{2} &= 16 \\\\\nv_{1} &= ± 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration.\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $4\\,\\rm{m/s}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "999c4d55-0cbb-5dcc-a421-563bcdceed78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178181} {:answer/id #uuid "0d2dfd7e-0b27-55db-809f-ca51f98cc0cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178182} {:answer/id #uuid "43f22f78-4a1e-5622-9b3f-47a164b39748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178183} {:answer/id #uuid "5407afe9-51b6-5c08-a6e4-c5884596940d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178184} {:answer/id #uuid "787f62d6-efa2-5ec5-ba5c-fec241cb9282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d2dfd7e-0b27-55db-809f-ca51f98cc0cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178182} :answer-field/id #uuid "00d967dc-6c93-55d9-bc06-7bec8bf5cf28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178180}] :question/id #uuid "8cf39bb1-9c63-5747-9e30-28aba079140a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163596" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $2x^{2}N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x = 0$ to $x = 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83958cd5-98b4-5471-a193-169760a3ee04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178205} {:answer/id #uuid "cf031512-cf61-58c0-8fd1-fe11a824b3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178206} {:answer/id #uuid "583c566d-6b5d-5150-8b94-779112bc705e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178207} {:answer/id #uuid "6314d0ec-acd7-5f07-994c-2182f8c3880e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178208} {:answer/id #uuid "78672ea8-12be-5075-8a76-f2e2057f7676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83958cd5-98b4-5471-a193-169760a3ee04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178205} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc01404c-6fba-52a3-9293-2058b5de8e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178204}] :question/id #uuid "c92a85dc-fea5-582a-a4d0-c749606f94b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163601" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $2tm/s$, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t = 1s$ and $t = 2s$."}]}
